WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.863
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.140
- Hello, welcome to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.780
It's my pleasure to welcome you all back.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.650
And I'm really honored today to host

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.430
what I think is a really
timely conversation,

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.080
especially as we're getting ready

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.460
on several college campuses
and university campuses

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.130
across the nation to go
back to our fall semester.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.670
Obviously, this is a fall
semester that, again, is different

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.530
from what we've experienced before.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.660
And so I think today's
conversation is very timely

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.580
as we think about campus life,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.410
as we think about mental health and faith

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.680
in our ongoing pandemic world.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.700
And I'm grateful for the guests
who are joining me today,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.130
these individuals that
I've known for a few years

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.190
and have been able to interact with

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.080
and partner with in different ways.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.840
And so I'm excited to be
able to share their words

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.390
with all of you and I'm
really looking forward

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.650
to our conversation and the
wisdom that I know they're going

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.060
to share with all of us as well.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.490
So the first guest I'd like to introduce

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:59.660
is Chaplain Sharon Kugler, and
Sharon has been the chaplain

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.260
at Yale University since 2007.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.930
She came to New Haven from
Johns Hopkins University

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.730
in Baltimore, where she had served

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.680
as the university chaplain since 1993.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.340
She has three decades of
experience in ministry

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.620
in higher education and
interfaith collaboration

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.240
and in pastoral and social ministry.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.090
Her main focus at Yale has
been cultivating chaplaincy

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.530
for students, faculty, and
staff, which defines itself

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.670
by serving the needs of the
richly diverse religious

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.910
and spiritual traditions on campus,

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.540
allowing for deeper dialogue,
increased accessibility,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.150
personal growth, creative
educational opportunities,

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.040
and pastoral leadership.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.540
And Sharon is also one who has served

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.030
in several different leadership roles

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.470
in national chaplaincy organizations.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.320
So I'm grateful and honored
to welcome Sharon Kugler

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.860
to the Addy Hour podcast today.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.650
Thanks so much for being here.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.800
- It's my pleasure.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:53.633
It really is.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.830
- The second guest I
would like to introduce,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.170
is Orlando Yarborough III.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.490
Orlando is a scientist,
certified leadership coach,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.240
consultant, speaker, and trainer.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.990
He coaches and trains a
diverse international portfolio

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.870
of young professionals,
academics, and community,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.410
and business leaders
in leader development,

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.320
professional and communication
skills and personal growth.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.610
He also pastors the Black Church at Yale,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.030
also known as BCAY,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.730
which is a student-run
church on Yale's campus

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.400
that has been in existence since 1972.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.960
Dr. Orlando is a Meyerhoff
scholar who holds a BA in Biology

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.960
and Africana studies from
the University of Maryland,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.750
Baltimore County and a PhD in
genetics from Yale University.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.960
He also holds certificates
in marriage counseling

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.370
and pastoral leadership, and is certified

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.510
as a Maxwell DISC Method
behavioral consultant

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.440
and Maxwell parenting and family guide.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.080
So deeply honored to
welcome Orlando Yarborough

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.850
to the Addy Hour podcast as well.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.070
Thanks for being here, Orlando.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:57.903
- Thank you.

00:02:57.903 --> 00:02:58.780
It's my honor to be here.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.040
- The third guest, which
I'd like to introduce

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.980
is Omer Bajwa, who is the
director of Muslim life

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.860
in the chaplain's office at
Yale, and who has been engaged

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.710
in religious service and
inter-religious engagement

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.470
and educational outreach since 2000.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.220
He holds degrees and has a certificate

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.040
in Islamic chaplaincy.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.190
He also has an MA in Near Eastern Studies

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.470
and an MS In communications.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.230
He is someone who has studied
several classical Islamic

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.820
sciences with traditional
scholars from Pakistan, Turkey,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.410
and the United States.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.960
His interests include
Islam in the United States

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.930
and the intersections of culture, media,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.920
politics, and spirituality.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.670
He regularly lectures about these topics

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:44.200
and others around the country
and he is also the co editor

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.387
of a forthcoming book

00:03:45.387 --> 00:03:49.450
"Mantle of Mercy: Islamic
Chaplaincy in North America".

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.260
So thank you Omer for also being here

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.610
and joining us on the
Addy hour podcast today.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.470
- Thank you, it's a profound privilege.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.303
Thank you.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.300
- Well, again, I'm grateful
to all of you for being here.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.580
I just have to start by
saying how grateful I am

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.053
for the work that you all do on campus,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.070
investing in students and
really walking with them

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.390
in their spiritual journeys

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.730
and especially with all the topics

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.570
that we're going to be
talking about today.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.410
Obviously, that's always important work

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.260
and even so much more
so, and all of the things

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.590
that we're experiencing as a
society these days as well.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:23.790
And even before we start, I
think, we're gonna be talking

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.050
about important topics, but
also just want to, of course,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.380
acknowledge that there are
challenges that are going on

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.750
all over the world sometimes
things that get more press

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.250
and other times, obviously,
the events in Afghanistan.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.450
But just acknowledging that
these challenges are things

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.060
that are going on on an ongoing basis

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.570
and wants to be just acknowledging
and cognizant of that,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.230
even as we're having the
conversation today too.

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.650
But knowing that this is in
the context of that as well.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.720
I just wanted to make sure
that our audiences knows

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.630
that we're thinking about
those things as well

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.390
and just the challenges and suffering

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.810
that lots of people are
going through in many ways.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.650
At the same time, it also
gives me hope, just being here

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.940
on this screen, on the
airways with all of you

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.730
and knowing that we're all in
this fight and this struggle

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.843
and this joy of life together.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.130
So with that, just want to
start out as I often do,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.330
just checking in with all of you

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.430
to see how you're doing these days.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.040
This is obviously, again,
another unique semester

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.040
that we're entering into
on our college campuses.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.230
And just want to see how
you all are doing overall,

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.350
both in your personal
lives and in your roles

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.510
and interactions with students.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:28.410
So Sharon, if we could start with you.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.290
- Sure, thank you.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:35.940
I think, I wanna first
say, I ended last semester

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.480
as exhausted as I think I'd ever been

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.290
in my professional career.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.880
Just, what's the expression?

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.113
Tired to the bone.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:49.130
And so the month of July,
I took a technology break

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:54.113
and that was very helpful
to step away from the email,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.170
still doing some work, but
just really putting a pause

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:02.130
on Zoom and all those things
because I was frankly nervous

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.640
about the next year,
about this new semester

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.010
that starts in just two
weeks, and that did help.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.820
It made a difference
and it gave me a chance

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:17.440
to have some meditative time.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.970
And I've been doing a lot of thinking

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.660
about what our students have experienced,

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.150
how they're trying to make sense

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.763
of what they're coming back to.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:32.120
The anxiety levels are
very high everywhere,

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.610
but especially with young
adults, it's we know this,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:41.380
the studies have shown the
increase in these challenges.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:46.380
So I am also aware that
I can't fix it all,

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.720
it's not my role to fix it all,

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.490
but I can witness it and tell the truth.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:54.710
That's what I'm trying to do.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.650
We had a staff retreat last week,

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.450
a day-long retreat together.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.500
Omer was there and part of
the theme of the retreat

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.900
was there's healing in the return.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.950
And so we're talking about that.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:11.652
That's the words of the Buddhist
teacher, Sharon Salzberg.

00:07:11.652 --> 00:07:15.310
And what does it mean to
return and what does it mean

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.140
to be part of that healing?

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.860
So that's kind of where my
head and my heart is right now,

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.853
as we look to the fall.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.130
- Well, thanks for your honesty
and I appreciate you sharing

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.417
that reflection time as well

00:07:27.417 --> 00:07:29.830
and the ways that you were
identifying with your students

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.250
and understanding what
they're going through,

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.760
what we're all going through.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.970
I'm curious if it was
difficult to pull away,

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:39.950
even at the beginning, even
as you had set that in motion,

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:42.640
how did that process come about?

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.190
- Well, you really have to give
yourself permission to do it

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:49.488
because honestly, it's muscle memory.

00:07:49.488 --> 00:07:53.940
I mean, I don't sleep with
my phone on the bed table.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.740
That is one flight of stairs away

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.330
and that's been a good
habit that I've kept...

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.710
Really, I've kept that up ever
since I've had a smartphone

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:09.770
and it's made a difference so
that I don't have that reflex

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.060
when I wake up in the morning.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.940
But it's the first thing I
pick up when I go downstairs.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.780
So I had to really pay attention to that

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.180
and also ask myself the question

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.130
when I'm opening my computer,
what is it that you're doing?

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.530
Are you writing something great?

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:29.940
Are you reading something
that's been written

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.410
and isn't sort of a social media thing?

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:33.530
Awesome. (laughing)

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:37.399
So I would say it took probably four days

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:39.339
to (mumbles) discipline.

00:08:39.339 --> 00:08:40.218
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:40.218 --> 00:08:41.370
I think that's helpful to hear as well.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:44.250
I mean, even as you're describing
the phone being a flight

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.260
of stairs away, I'm sure
many people kind of recoil,

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.930
oh, ooh, just kinda that,

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:51.900
wow, can the phone
really be that far away?

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.270
What if I miss something important?

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:54.920
So I think you're exactly right.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:58.333
And it sounds like, in a way
you've you set up a discipline.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.490
But even with that, it sounds
like it still took time.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:03.090
So I think that's really important

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:03.923
that you were able to do that.

00:09:03.923 --> 00:09:06.340
I think that's a good
principle too 'cause I know

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:07.960
with students that I've interacted with,

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.200
that's been something
we've talked about a lot,

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.637
just giving yourself that time.

00:09:11.637 --> 00:09:14.660
The tendency is to day one
say, "Oh, I couldn't do it.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:15.493
It didn't work."

00:09:15.493 --> 00:09:18.230
And then to get stuck and then
just start to beat oneself up

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.560
and kind of get stuck in the cycle.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.700
So I appreciate your candor in that too

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.930
and taking us through
that process as well, so.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.173
What about you, Orlando?

00:09:28.222 --> 00:09:32.210
How's the process been for
you in terms of everything

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:34.620
that we've experienced
as you're getting ready

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:35.720
for the fall semester?

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.310
- Yes, thank you.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.850
At the beginning of August,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.760
I got a chance to take a technology break

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.440
and spend time with family
in a different state.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.020
And that was refreshing.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:52.920
It wasn't planned, so I don't necessarily

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.890
say planned technology
breaks though I should,

00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:00.140
but this was one by circumstance
and it just helped me

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:05.140
to be in the moment, to
be present and to say,

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:09.940
okay, I'm away and I can connect

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.470
in a different way with people.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:14.780
I think as I enter this
semester, in terms of my role

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.123
as pastor of BCAY, I'm worn
and we haven't even started.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.423
And I think I'm worn in terms of strategy.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.220
I don't necessarily know
that there's an expectation

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.720
for leadership to have answers,

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.640
but certainly the time
where you need a leader

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.410
is during the time when
there's a lot of uncertainty.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.780
And I'm just recognizing

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:40.730
that I can't plan that far ahead and...

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.343
Yes, can't play that for ahead,

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.760
maybe not even a couple of weeks ahead

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.500
and I don't have all the answers.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.580
And so the question is,
what are the values?

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.050
Or what are the principles?

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:57.130
What are the spiritual
practices or even the goals

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:59.160
that I may wanna hold on to

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.580
and may want to encourage our members

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.020
and our students to hold to?

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:09.020
So I think in terms of being
a strategic leader, I'm worn

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.460
because I'm not coming up
with long-term strategy

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:14.910
and I think that's appropriate
for me not to do so.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.263
We've seen that long-term
planning doesn't stay.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:24.400
And at the same time, I
am hopeful and challenged

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:29.780
about the idea of having
spiritual community on campus

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:31.490
or spiritual community that's connected

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.630
with the academic start of the year.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:40.630
If there's any time to bring
a wholeness to who I am,

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:45.150
to who our communities are,
this is the time to do it.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.730
So I think my being a part
of this work and being a part

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:52.810
of the larger fabric of Yale University,

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.593
and of campus ministry,

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:58.833
we're made for this time.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.320
And so that gives me a lot
of hope and encouragement.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.020
And I'm looking forward
to just being the person

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.163
for this hour and connecting
with other people,

00:12:10.163 --> 00:12:13.333
with students who are
the people for this hour.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:15.357
- That's really encouraging to hear.

00:12:15.357 --> 00:12:18.117
And I appreciate your level
of transparency as well.

00:12:18.117 --> 00:12:21.110
And I think I'm sure this has
likely been your experience

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.930
by offering me that level of transparency

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:26.060
is healing in a sense
too, as our students know

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:27.470
that we don't have all the answers

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:29.470
and as they hear us
walking through it as well,

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:33.190
and as they see us also leaning
on each other and on them

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.260
as community members,

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.730
and as you talked about
the spirit of community,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.540
I think that goes such a long way.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:40.190
And I think Glod Jr. was
on the podcast previously

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.180
a couple of weeks ago and one
thing that he talked about,

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:45.480
which you reminded me about
was just the wholeness

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.440
that can come from brokenness as well,

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.440
because he talked about
the wholeness of community,

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:52.210
but not to say that there
aren't cracks in the jar,

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:53.760
so to speak, as he mentioned.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:55.750
And so even as you are
trying to figure out

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:56.890
what the strategies look like,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:57.900
you haven't figured it all out,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.100
but you're able to lean on
community and to have a sense

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.820
of wholeness with the
community in that space.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.070
And I think that's really powerful.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.780
And I would imagine that
your students get strength

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:09.130
from that as well as they see
you walk through that journey.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:10.770
So I appreciate you sharing that.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.430
- Yes, thank you.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.623
Omer, what about for you?

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:18.610
- Thank you to-
- (mumbles) and reengaging.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.390
- My friends and colleagues,
it's just fantastic answers.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:24.400
I think for me, there's been
sort of this juxtaposition

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.650
or this tension almost between
two overriding feelings.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.940
One is, to be very honest amongst friends,

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.460
in many ways I felt myself really

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.383
in the last year and a half
able to breathe, right,

00:13:36.383 --> 00:13:39.090
with just the forced slow down, right?

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:40.930
The nature of our work in the academy,

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:44.750
in these spaces, in academic
spaces, in community spaces

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:46.010
is that we're always on.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.400
And I just felt like
for me, very personally,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.110
I spent a lot more quality
time with my family.

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:55.200
I was able to revisit a lot
of my spiritual disciplines

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.900
and practices that I had that sort of

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.170
that way you wanna get back into.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:04.930
And then, of the blessings I
would say of that is I had time

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:06.740
where the outside world was shut off

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.170
and then I got to do writing,
which I'd always wanted to do

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.340
when I sat down with my friends

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.210
and we worked on this
book project together,

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.720
which is forthcoming, as
you mentioned, thank you.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.260
And so that in that way,
I feel incredibly grateful

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:21.380
and blessed to the almighty
for everything that I got.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:26.010
At the same time, I'm
mindful of the world has been

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.110
and continues to be a place of deep sorrow

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:32.100
and tension and brokenness
as we've witnessed

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.113
at so many levels.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:37.400
But at a personal level right
now too, is I am full of,

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:40.960
as Orlando said too, the
uncertainty of the fall semester,

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.840
like I'm just finding
myself day after day,

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.410
we're getting closer to the
opening of the semester.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.390
And I just have this unease

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:52.170
and anxiety with like the unknown,

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:53.590
like, what's it gonna be like?

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:57.630
And I understand things keep
changing, we have to be nimble,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.510
we have to be flexible
with this, but I mean,

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.350
what I think a year ago
where I was feeling,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.480
it was very different place.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.370
But thank you for asking.

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:07.530
- Well, that's encouraging
to hear as well,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:10.500
just a refreshment and as
you've named, just the tension

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:11.910
that's still there as well.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:13.800
I'm also curious if you talked
about some of the spiritual

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.690
disciplines and the writing
you've been able to do,

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:18.810
even amidst the anxiety
that you're feeling

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:22.520
as we all come back to campus, in a sense.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.410
How do you anticipate
those moving forward?

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:27.350
Do you think those disciplines will stick?

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:28.183
Are you starting to...

00:15:28.183 --> 00:15:29.523
Are you gonna see how it goes?

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:32.390
It sounds like you had
the space to create them.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:35.430
And is that space gonna still
be there as you go forward?

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.510
'Cause I imagine that's
something that a lot of students

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.240
will be thinking about as well.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.073
- Yeah. No, thank you.

00:15:40.073 --> 00:15:41.620
I mean, that's the million dollar question

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:43.450
that I ask myself every day, right?

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:44.930
Is that I want to...

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.640
There's days when I think
I got this, I can do this,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.050
I can maintain it.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.260
Once you be healthy, once you
be balanced, how they do it.

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.930
There's other days where I'm just like,

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.780
oh no, something's got to get jettisoned

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.520
over the edge, right, of
the ship to keep it upright.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.970
So (mumbles) go back
and forth day after day.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.950
But I'd like to think I'm surrounded

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:07.050
by incredibly supportive
people that one of the things

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.570
that I've sort of tried to
remind myself throughout this

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.770
is that no man is an
island, right, so to speak.

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:12.890
I can't do it alone.

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:16.410
All of the support that I've
had has come from family,

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.470
friends, colleagues that has allowed me

00:16:19.338 --> 00:16:20.856
to flourish in my own way, so.

00:16:20.856 --> 00:16:21.850
And I'm going to be mindful of that.

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:23.800
And so it's just about shifting,

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.670
we're gonna re-engage
with community, right?

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:28.060
With our friends, with our
colleagues, with our students.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.070
And so it's about re-navigating that.

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.200
- Yup, right.

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:32.033
Yeah. Yeah.

00:16:32.033 --> 00:16:33.210
I really appreciate that

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.890
and reminds me of what Sharon
mentioned too, in this topic

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:38.410
that you all had talked
about in the return,

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:39.850
the coming back and the returning.

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:43.000
And I'm curious if any of you
are willing to share some of,

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:44.800
if there are any principles
that have come out

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:46.660
of those discussions, even the retreat

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:50.580
that you all had together
in terms of ways to return,

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.570
or maybe things to try and be aware of

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:56.790
along any of these aspects in
terms of spiritual practices,

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.850
in terms of mental health,
just in terms of navigation.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:02.363
So anyone that's willing
to kind of share maybe some

00:17:02.363 --> 00:17:04.013
of the conversations around that.

00:17:05.445 --> 00:17:09.100
- I think Omer and Orlando
both touched upon this

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:09.933
in different ways,

00:17:09.933 --> 00:17:12.633
but we're living with
contradictions, right?

00:17:13.484 --> 00:17:17.750
So there's the built-in
kind of anxiety and worry

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:20.953
that we feel of the unknown.

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:25.090
And we've been living with
the unknown for so long now

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:29.630
that sometimes the word
doesn't even have the power

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:32.310
that we know it actually has,

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:33.840
but we just sort of glide over it.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.240
There's so much unknown.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.413
Well, that means there's so much loss.

00:17:38.922 --> 00:17:40.560
There's so much uncertainty,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.590
these are all big, heavy things.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.310
At the same time, there
are also these joys

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:49.560
that are they're dancing
in the middle of it too,

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.600
we're seeing each other again,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:55.460
we're planning for our kind of a future.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.300
We're not sure what it's gonna look like,

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:00.110
but we're trying to put our
heads together and figure out

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:03.050
how can we pull off this ritual?

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.730
How can we do this or that?

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:09.110
And that I think is feeding us,

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:11.360
but I think what came out of our retreat

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.600
and certainly Omer, please chime in,

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.490
was also just a willingness to say,

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:19.930
there's just a lot of mess ahead.

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:23.300
And we have to be kind to
ourselves and each other

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.043
as we sort of trump through it.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.550
And that's just us, the adults.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:32.030
So you can imagine how
students are going to be.

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:36.180
And they live a life that
is a lot faster paced.

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:40.220
I was just talking with
a friend the other day

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:44.680
about students on their phones
are having probably five

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:46.900
or six different kinds of conversations

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:48.640
with different individuals in their lives

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:49.773
all at the same time.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:52.490
They could be texting
with a family member,

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.330
they could be texting with a
professor, with two friends,

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.570
with somebody they wanna be friends with.

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:59.330
And then they're also filming themselves

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:01.240
and they're doing all
kinds of other things.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:05.970
And so there's a different
energy that students have

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:10.040
as they walk through the
world that we as chaplains

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.670
and imams and ministers are witnessing

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:18.680
and trying to help them also step back

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:21.780
and kind of gain an
agency over themselves.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.530
And so I think about the
beginning of the school year,

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:27.740
and normally our offices would be crowded

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:30.700
with students coming by to
say hello and get an ice cream

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:32.470
and all those things.

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:35.440
And now, we're gonna be there,
but we're gonna be masked

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:37.068
and they can't loiter and...

00:19:37.068 --> 00:19:39.630
(laughing)

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:44.630
It's all gonna be a kind of
messy, albeit loving something.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:47.570
And we just don't quite
know what it'll be,

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:52.570
but what I'm most excited
about is providing some kind

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:56.550
of soft landing for these
students that are processing

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:59.360
so much both in their personal lives,

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:02.510
but also in their
academic and social lives.

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:03.343
So yeah.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:05.560
- Wow. That was really well said.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:07.670
And even though it was probably not meant

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:09.520
as a profound word, even
the way you just said it,

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:11.600
it'll be something.
(laughing)

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:15.650
I think that that captures
it, all the different pieces

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:16.530
and all the elements.

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:18.150
We don't know what all
of that will look like,

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:19.560
but it will still be something

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:22.163
and that something will
still be meaningful.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.680
- Yeah, if I may, just one
more thing, I think that,

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.380
and I appreciate you
catching that, as a leader,

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:33.080
I'm often asked questions that
I just don't have answers to

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.290
and I feel horrible about it.

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:38.557
But I do know there will be something.

00:20:38.557 --> 00:20:40.190
(laughing)

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:41.450
- That's so true.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:42.670
- Yeah.

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.280
- Omer, anything you want to add there

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:46.720
from your perspective?
- Yeah, no, thank you, Sharon.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:48.250
Fantastic.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:49.150
Very little to add.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:51.070
I mean, I think one of the thoughts

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.112
that I keep coming back to,

00:20:53.112 --> 00:20:56.920
and we talked about
their treat as well is...

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:58.480
And I'm guilty of this as well, right?

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:00.470
The language of, when are
we gonna return to normal?

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:01.530
When will things go back to normal?

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.960
Like normal classes,
normal social interactions

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:04.793
and all of that.

00:21:04.793 --> 00:21:06.660
And look, just as much as the next person,

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:08.670
I'm pining for that, right?

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:11.890
But I think, one of the
things that I remind myself

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:16.890
is to just really shift the
lens in a sense that say,

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:19.850
well, of the many lessons
that maybe the pandemic

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:22.990
and the global slowdown
has taught us is that

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:25.990
what was normal before and
normal may have worked for some,

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:27.510
but it didn't work for many, right?

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.680
In terms of race relations,
in terms of healthcare,

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:33.020
in terms of education, in
terms of predatory economies,

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:34.970
and the list goes on and on, right?

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:39.290
And so, I mean, I don't have
the answer except to say that,

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:41.373
is that the normal I wanna go back to?

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:47.770
Or can we try to create a
different normal or world, right?

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.930
That's something that this
idea of healing and return

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:55.090
as we're being invited
to return to a semblance

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:57.040
of life together.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:58.110
And how do we heal, right?

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:01.710
The fractures as the last
year has taught us, right?

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:03.360
With the massive social upheavals

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:07.730
and we're witnessing sort
of horrible climate change

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.190
that's accelerating in front of our eyes

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:10.140
and the list goes on, right?

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:10.973
I just try to...

00:22:10.973 --> 00:22:12.820
I mean, I try to, at least with my kids,

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.020
we try to talk about
this from time to time,

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:16.210
is that they're all geared up.

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:17.120
We're going back to school.

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:18.480
It's gonna be normal again.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.380
Well, let's slow down and think about

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:21.580
what does normal mean for you and I?

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:23.220
what does it mean for
people around the world?

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:25.000
So I just put that out there.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:26.980
- Yeah, that's a really important point.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.700
And what the new normal will look like.

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:31.020
To follow up with that a little bit,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.500
I'm curious from the lens
from all three of you,

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:36.570
what you've seen shift
in terms of campus life

00:22:37.567 --> 00:22:40.120
since the pandemic started
and what you're seeing shift

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.300
for the good and what you've
seen maybe shift for the bad

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:44.410
and ways that we're
trying to address that.

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:45.670
Orlando, maybe we can start with you

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:49.120
if you have any perspectives there.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.310
- Yes, I think a positive shift

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:55.210
might be a greater
appreciation for relationships,

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:58.450
a greater appreciation
for who's in front of you.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:02.800
I can imagine students not
wanting to break apart (laughing)

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.240
end their evening conversation
or leave the group

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:07.910
that they're a part of.

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:12.440
I can imagine some professors
just really appreciating

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:14.610
more deeply being in the classroom

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.290
and interacting with students.

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:20.620
There's a ease to being in
person in terms of communication,

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:25.110
in terms of timing that we
can take breaths together

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:29.220
and that we can pause and people
can still follow each other

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:32.320
in terms of what's being thought

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:34.220
or what's being communicated.

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:36.560
And so I think there's gonna
be a heightened appreciation

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.173
for being onsite and in the
presence of one another.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:46.440
And I also think on the
challenges side, there might be...

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.633
I think dependencies may come out.

00:23:53.068 --> 00:23:57.170
I think there may be some
unhealthy revelations of behavior

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:02.230
or desires that will
allow healing to then come

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:03.380
because of that revelation.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:05.230
But I think that's going to come out.

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:08.010
Something during the pandemic,

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:11.230
many people on campus felt isolated

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:12.700
because even though they were on campus,

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.993
they weren't necessarily all together.

00:24:15.838 --> 00:24:18.750
And so you take two isolated
people who want to be together

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:20.930
and they're hanging out
maybe more than they should,

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:22.270
or there's increased desire.

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:25.630
So I think there'll be a
little maybe overcompensation

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:29.130
or overcorrection in terms
of what happened last year,

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:32.250
what's been happening over the
summer and the opportunities

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:35.870
that will present
themselves this coming year.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:37.690
I think it will be mostly positive

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:40.913
and everything will be an
opportunity for further growth.

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:43.890
- Yeah, and again, I
keep isolating that word,

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:46.960
but I think growth is so well
set on that aspect as well

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.980
because even as you talked
about some of the positives

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:51.610
and some of the areas
where there'll be tensions

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:53.240
and opportunities for learning,

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.847
it seems like that is exactly what it is.

00:24:55.847 --> 00:24:57.310
And then to go back to what Sharon said,

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:59.770
to have grace for some of those missteps

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:02.600
or challenges along the way
and to make sure there's room

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.610
for that 'cause it's a journey
that we're all in together.

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:07.643
Definitely appreciate you
sharing both of those insights.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:09.693
- Thank you, yeah.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:13.300
- And then, I mean, you've
also, as we've been talking,

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.170
you've all of you and myself included,

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:18.540
I think we can all relate
to the anxiety component

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.880
that's been there and classmates
we talked about anxiety

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:24.880
kind of that creeps up when
there is either something

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.400
that's unknown or a perception
of things being unknown.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:29.360
And there's a lot of true
to that in this moment

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:30.890
that we're in right now.

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:33.560
So also, in your
interactions with students,

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:36.810
I'm curious how you have
seen and how you've walked

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:39.480
with students in terms of
their emotional journeys

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:40.313
and their mental health.

00:25:40.313 --> 00:25:42.400
How have you seen that play out?

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:44.870
I would anticipate that
it's been a mixture,

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:47.550
but would love to hear
how that process has been.

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:49.580
And then what you take
from those experiences

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.350
as you head into this new semester

00:25:51.350 --> 00:25:53.420
and continue to think about
how you support students

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:54.540
in that way.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.360
Omer, maybe we can start
with you this time around.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:59.040
- Sure, thank you.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:00.070
Two thoughts come to mind.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:05.070
One is related to the point
is that students are so hungry

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:08.820
for community and connection, right?

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:11.420
I mean, literally, I open
my email every morning

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:14.210
and I've got a different student
undergrad, post-doc like,

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:17.180
"When are we gonna start
Friday prayers again on campus?

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.520
When can we see each other?

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:22.670
When is the student life aspect

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.400
of campus gonna pick up again?"

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:26.367
And the answer is always,

00:26:26.367 --> 00:26:29.800
"I don't know, but God willing, soon

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:31.180
and let's just be patient."

00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:32.013
Right?

00:26:32.013 --> 00:26:34.170
And I think that relates
to the second point

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:36.280
about the mental health is that I think

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:39.220
there aren't any easy answers to this

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.280
to navigate with students.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:44.190
But the thing that I have
really been able to stress

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:47.050
in a lot of my one-on-ones
with students and that's,

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:50.080
in my mind, one of the
interesting sort of things

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:51.130
that I discovered with this

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:54.860
is that I'm all about in-person
interaction being present,

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.330
being present with the other person.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:59.530
But I found myself doing a
lot more one-on-ones, right,

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:03.020
with students and community
members during this.

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:06.420
And one of the things that's
come out is, in a strange way,

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:09.120
we've been able to go deeper, right,

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:10.990
in some of these sessions and with that,

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:12.840
ones that are struggled
with mental health,

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:16.710
I've been able to really spend
time and I'm not a therapist,

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:18.200
I make that very clear, right?

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:20.210
But to be able to refer them to sources.

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:21.690
And I think it's gotten
them thinking, right,

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:22.523
that they have this...

00:27:22.523 --> 00:27:25.080
Because they've slowed down,
it's almost like that space

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:28.030
has been created in their
life and in their work life

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:30.560
to be like, "Yeah, I've
kind of put this off

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:32.390
and I'm really struggling and I need this.

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:33.350
I need to find someone.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.620
So can you help me find
someone or what to do

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.220
or what to read or who to talk to.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:41.150
And that has been sort of a
pleasant surprise, I may say,

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:43.234
from this in my experience.

00:27:43.234 --> 00:27:45.080
- That's great to hear
and refreshing as well

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:46.490
and appreciate you being there.

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:48.210
It seems like you've
helped facilitate that

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.350
as well as a combination
of both the circumstance

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:51.330
and the openness to it.

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:54.250
So that's really, really powerful.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:56.710
Sharon, what about you
from your perspective

00:27:56.710 --> 00:28:00.710
as the lead chaplain, in a
sense, and what the oversight

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:03.320
that you have and the
interactions with students?

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:07.720
- Well, I have to say, certainly,

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:12.100
our schedules really were
much more busy, much fuller.

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.630
We were going at a slower
pace, but we were going,

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:18.600
I felt like at a fuller
gate with the amount

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.160
of students we were seeing over Zoom.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:26.020
And one of the things that
became really clear early on,

00:28:26.020 --> 00:28:28.350
these were students that I did not know

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:32.870
that were reaching out because
they felt something was off

00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:34.200
for them or they were frightened

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.790
about how they were feeling.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:38.290
And we were able to get
to the heart of the matter

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:40.610
so much more quickly.

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:43.500
And it only occurred to
me that that was happening

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:48.090
maybe about halfway into
the year where I'm thinking,

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:49.770
wow, they didn't even know me.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:54.760
And when I asked a sort of open question,

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:56.850
they would go right in.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:29:00.170
And so what that made me think
about was the distractions

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:05.170
in my office, the things that
could buy time before getting

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:07.730
to the heart of the
matter, whether it's...

00:29:07.730 --> 00:29:09.540
I have a snow globe collection

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:11.570
and I have goofy little windup toys.

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:14.780
And I thought to myself,
yeah, they only had just this

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:16.510
to look at.

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:21.140
And so I have thought about
keeping some hours open

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:24.260
over Zoom so that people
could make an appointment.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:27.330
Let's say they just didn't feel
like walking across campus,

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.020
but they wanna talk to somebody.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:30.020
And so I do...

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:31.560
We've talked about this as a staff.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:34.233
We are gonna hang on to some of that.

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:38.430
A couple other colleagues

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:42.400
in our office did anti-racists book groups

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:47.220
and they had people coming
to these book groups

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.080
that would never have been able to do it,

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:51.910
but they just had to push a
button and they were there

00:29:51.910 --> 00:29:54.410
and they could eat their
lunch and then push the button

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:56.060
and go back to whatever they were doing.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:30:01.060
And so we see some sort of
important aha moments in that,

00:30:01.857 --> 00:30:04.187
that I think that the technology

00:30:04.187 --> 00:30:07.920
that we're all forced to
use, isn't all that bad

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.900
in some cases.
(laughing)

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:11.920
And so there's that.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.850
And I think specifically with
some of these conversations,

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:18.530
I can remember, oh my goodness,
the very first student

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:23.530
who made an appointment to see
me when we went into lockdown

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:26.977
and his first question was,
"Is this the end of the world?"

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:31.890
And in all my years in chaplaincy,

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:35.033
no one had asked me that
question quite that way.

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:39.597
And I said, "It's the end of a world."

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:44.410
And I felt like, gosh, I
hope I didn't freak him out

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:45.243
by saying that.

00:30:45.243 --> 00:30:47.570
But I also felt it was
important to tell the truth.

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:49.720
And that's what I was
feeling in that moment.

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:54.720
And I think that's true that
it was the end of a world.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:31:00.430
And so our work is, what does
this next world need to be

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:05.430
so that we are better
in it and better for it?

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:09.880
So these deep conversations,

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:12.560
some of them kind of
existential and broad,

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:15.790
and some of them just a
person is personally terrified

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:20.660
to leave their house have been present

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:25.550
in just such a high volume for all of us.

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:28.670
And I think the last thing
I'll say is just as the sort

00:31:28.670 --> 00:31:32.220
of person that has to
keep everybody afloat

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:37.220
in terms of the other chaplains,
I just felt so humbled

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:39.950
by how we were, when one person was down,

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:42.910
someone else stepped in,
and it was just like...

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:46.980
We didn't choreograph it that
way, but I think all of us,

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:49.630
all the different religious
and spiritual organizations

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:54.630
just knew this is hard
and we have to keep going,

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:57.393
but we don't have to be perfect.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:01.827
- It's so important.

00:32:03.012 --> 00:32:04.549
And I'm encouraged just what the...

00:32:04.549 --> 00:32:07.060
I mean, the theme that keeps
coming up is the opportunity

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:09.300
that's been there in a lot of ways.

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.410
And it's great to know that
you're keeping both of those

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:13.880
in-person and Zoom options available

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:16.280
to create more opportunity
in some ways as well.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.880
- I think it will lead
us to important insights

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:24.150
into how other ways to do this work.

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:29.150
And honestly, the key has
always been, in ministry,

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:31.213
not to abandon.

00:32:32.380 --> 00:32:34.730
Now we're in a situation
where we physically

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:37.660
couldn't be together, but
we had to think of ways

00:32:37.660 --> 00:32:40.603
to help people not feel
like they were abandoned.

00:32:40.603 --> 00:32:41.436
- Right.

00:32:41.436 --> 00:32:43.083
- And that they're suffering alone.

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:48.150
And so, again, it was an imperfect thing,

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:50.990
but it did give us some stuff, I think,

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:52.880
that we have to pay attention to.

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:53.713
- Yeah.

00:32:53.713 --> 00:32:54.546
Yeah.

00:32:54.546 --> 00:32:55.379
And creative community.

00:32:55.379 --> 00:32:57.440
I think even the way you've
described the students,

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:59.160
I mean, I feel like I
have to give them a lot

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:03.190
of encouragement and support
for just even taking advantage.

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:04.023
I mean, you said you're
hearing from students

00:33:04.023 --> 00:33:05.740
that you never heard from before.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:09.060
So taking a step out of a
comfort zone to reach out

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:11.140
and to connect, and it sounds
like those conversations

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:12.610
are going very deep pretty quickly.

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:16.270
So again, the opportunity in
that community and encouraging

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:18.380
to hear even the way
amongst the chaplains,

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:20.130
that you've been able to
step in for each other.

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:23.140
I think that speaks well
about your community as well

00:33:23.140 --> 00:33:26.060
in the ways that you all
are supporting each other

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:28.023
in these different ways, so.

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:32.473
Orlando for you as a pastor
of the Black Church of Yale,

00:33:32.473 --> 00:33:35.150
have you seen some of these
same principles at play?

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:36.820
Are there other ones that have come up

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:38.090
that we haven't quite touched on as well

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:40.020
in terms of the students'
emotional journeys

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:41.920
and just support around mental health?

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.450
- Yeah, so I'm seeing a lot of overlap.

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:48.490
I think for me, there's
been a dream come true,

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:52.290
something that I've desired
for many years, and that is

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:59.820
ongoing conversation
and just an opportunity

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:03.690
for a continuity of experience.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:07.720
And over this pandemic,
it's been nice to be able

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:12.720
to have ongoing conversations
about a student's wellbeing

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.270
or about what they're thinking,
what they're experiencing,

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:19.730
what they're dreaming about,
or what their concern is

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:22.087
versus just you speak to somebody once

00:34:22.087 --> 00:34:24.810
and you don't hear from them again.

00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:27.030
And so something that I...

00:34:27.030 --> 00:34:28.510
It's a desire because there's...

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.060
It's usually one-on-one
and so I'm trying to think,

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:33.700
well, how do we multiply this?

00:34:33.700 --> 00:34:35.160
But is the opportunity

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:40.160
to really have someone
walk beside a student,

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:43.270
like a shepherd would walk beside sheep,

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:46.650
like someone really to take
them from one place to another

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:49.360
and be there for conversation,
be there for dialogue,

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:53.260
or just be there to listen,
which is mostly what happens.

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:57.400
Me being able to be quiet and
just hear and to ask questions

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:01.490
that will serve the student
and will help bring reflection

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:05.160
and revelation, but also
just maybe greater clarity

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:07.130
on where they are, or just the assurance

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:11.240
that you're not alone even if
you may feel that you're alone

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:14.060
in certain ways, I'm also here with you.

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:16.470
And then the second
thing that has happened

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:20.490
that I think Sharon has
really demonstrated,

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:23.000
so Sharon has this wonderful
opportunity to be a leader

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:27.700
of leaders, leading the
chaplain office chaplains,

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:30.100
but then also leading the larger group

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:33.250
of religious ministers on campus.

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:37.620
And something that Sharon has
done, at the end of semester,

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.850
I realized there was an
opportunity for myself.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:42.150
So Sharon helps us to
get through the semester

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:45.423
and just helps us to say,
"Okay, this is what we're doing.

00:35:45.423 --> 00:35:46.730
This is what needs to happen."

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:48.820
And after we get through the semester,

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:51.460
Sharon, you help us get through the year,

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:53.123
get through the rest of the year.

00:35:54.778 --> 00:35:59.250
And it wasn't until the end of
last semester that I realized

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:04.250
that one of my goals or
one of the opportunities

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:08.270
that I had was just to
shepherd the students through

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.473
and to not lose any.

00:36:11.370 --> 00:36:12.820
And if there is a principle,

00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:15.890
maybe I'll call it the
Harriet Tubman Principle,

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:18.730
where you're leading a group
of people from one place

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:20.220
to another place, but you're helping them

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.070
to make that journey.

00:36:22.070 --> 00:36:25.760
And so when I come into
this semester this year,

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:27.210
one of the things I'm
thinking about is like,

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:31.110
okay, how do I help the students
who have begun this journey

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:33.430
to make it to the next stage?

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:35.870
And they don't have to make it perfectly.

00:36:35.870 --> 00:36:39.290
They don't have to make it
necessarily with all the goals

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.550
achieved that they've come
into the semester with.

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:44.690
But how do I help them to make it

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:47.897
and then cause them to look back and say,

00:36:47.897 --> 00:36:49.980
"See how far I've come?"

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:51.980
And see that we've come together.

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:55.790
And that's a wonderful opportunity,
is a wonderful blessing

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:57.573
to be a part of that process.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:03.760
And I think that's part
of something that helps

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:08.607
with mental health is being able to say,

00:37:08.607 --> 00:37:12.870
"We've done it once, we can do it again."

00:37:12.870 --> 00:37:15.040
Or, "Other people before me have done it.

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:17.540
Maybe I can do it too."

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:21.330
And that's something that I'm
looking forward to embracing

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:25.170
and honoring and cherishing
and rising to the occasion

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:27.570
to contribute to their own journeys

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:30.080
from one place to beginning place,

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:34.270
even if it's just the journey
from August to December

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:36.680
and then in January to May,

00:37:36.680 --> 00:37:38.350
and then of course over the summer

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:41.470
because we're still with
them in so many ways.

00:37:41.470 --> 00:37:44.250
- Yeah, that's so good and
it reverberates, as you said,

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:46.250
and as they're learning
from their own journeys

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:47.870
or looking to others as well and seeing

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:51.520
how that goes forward in
a lot of ways and my...

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:53.760
I'm trying not to go too
much into neuroscience,

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:55.810
but in some ways it thinks
about just the way our brain

00:37:55.810 --> 00:37:57.250
is designed as well.

00:37:57.250 --> 00:37:59.100
And the ways that our
brains respond to things

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:02.020
in our environment, how we're
really recreating those things

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:03.060
for us to perceive that.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:05.500
I mean, people talk about the memory cells

00:38:05.500 --> 00:38:07.660
and certain parts of the
brains in taxi drivers,

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:10.250
they're called mirror neurons,
which can kind of respond

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:12.150
to certain things in the environment.

00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:14.928
And even just what you're
talking about is...

00:38:14.928 --> 00:38:16.010
Might be getting too far outside

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:18.420
and making this parallel too
strong, but I'm just thinking

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:21.350
about the ways that we
can do that in our lives

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.270
and learn from that and grow from that

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:25.810
as communities as well.

00:38:25.810 --> 00:38:29.210
And so, yeah, just encouraged
to hear that approach

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:31.180
and for what you see coming forward

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:32.590
in this new semester as well.

00:38:32.590 --> 00:38:35.100
And I think you're all
highlighting some of the strengths,

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:37.870
it seems, that you all
have been able to galvanize

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:40.520
and that students have
also been using overall,

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:42.770
just in terms of emotional
wellness and mental health

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:45.450
and things that they
can use going forward.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:46.440
Not to make things negative,

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:49.810
but I'm also curious if
there've been misperceptions

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:51.220
that you've run into.

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:52.920
And I bring that up
because I think sometimes

00:38:52.920 --> 00:38:56.030
that can be clarifying to
speak about in larger contexts,

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:58.150
because I know in the events
that we've done in the past,

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:00.370
after we spoken about those
things in the public space,

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:02.660
people have said, "Oh, I've
been thinking about these things

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:05.840
for years, but I've never had
a place to talk about them."

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:07.720
So sometimes I think it's helpful for us

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:10.130
to voice those things and to be able

00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:13.470
to correct those misperceptions,
whether they're intentional

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:15.870
or unintentional and then
help us all think about ways

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:16.703
to move forward.

00:39:16.703 --> 00:39:18.980
So I'm just curious, as
you've been interacting

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:21.090
with students, if there
are certain misconceptions

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:23.210
about either spiritual
practices or mental health

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:25.880
that you've had to confront
in a gentle and loving way

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:27.777
to help students move to a better place.

00:39:27.777 --> 00:39:29.100
And they're just principles we can apply

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.200
from those things going forward.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:34.000
So Sharon, would you be willing
to just start with that?

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:36.263
- Sure.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:40.550
I'm always very candid with students

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:45.430
that I'm not a therapist and
that I will share with them

00:39:45.430 --> 00:39:46.970
when they're saying something

00:39:46.970 --> 00:39:50.700
that is making me frightened
for them, that again,

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:54.110
I won't abandon them.

00:39:54.110 --> 00:39:57.770
If I feel like we need extra support here,

00:39:57.770 --> 00:40:01.060
we need to go over, walk
over to mental health

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:04.120
and counseling, I'll stay with them.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:07.110
But I have found what's changed over time,

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:10.350
and this really isn't
related to the pandemic,

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:12.800
I think it's the time that we're in,

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:17.170
is that it's a lot easier to
talk about mental health issues

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:20.970
with students from across many religious

00:40:20.970 --> 00:40:21.990
and spiritual communities.

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:25.030
And I can remember a time
when it wasn't so easy

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:27.470
and that there was a lot of shame involved

00:40:27.470 --> 00:40:29.510
if it was ever kind of a conversation

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:33.120
about sharing a diagnosis
with a family member

00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:35.050
or a family member having a diagnosis

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:37.830
and then the student processing that

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:39.800
and not really knowing what to do with it.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:43.140
That has really changed
and changed for the better.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.360
And so now you can have these
conversations with a student.

00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:48.470
Let's say they have a counselor
and they're not comfortable

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:49.473
with that person.

00:40:50.620 --> 00:40:53.620
You can say, "Well, if you
didn't like your dentist,

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:54.840
you would change your dentist.

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:56.790
You want what's best for your teeth,

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:59.650
well, you want what's best
for your mental health."

00:40:59.650 --> 00:41:03.780
So when I first do a few
screening questions to find out

00:41:05.384 --> 00:41:08.170
is the fact that they
don't like the experience,

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:09.500
does it have to do with the fact

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:11.240
that they don't feel better yet?

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:13.240
And is that the therapist's fault

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:14.760
or is that part of the process?

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:17.983
So you kind of have to do a
little mining around that,

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:20.690
and I'm not sure if
this is getting exactly

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:23.070
to your question, but I
know that I'm just getting

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:26.550
a lot more frank with students
when we talk about this.

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:30.340
And the difference
between a mental illness

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:33.573
and a time of mental anguish or distress,

00:41:34.410 --> 00:41:38.650
whether it be from bad
news or a broken heart

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:41.170
or something like that
that's the stuff of life

00:41:41.170 --> 00:41:46.020
and making a distinction between the two

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:48.370
so that they can get the best help

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.790
and also understand that
they can survive it.

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:52.273
- Yeah, I think that's really helpful.

00:41:52.273 --> 00:41:53.150
I mean, even as someone

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:54.840
who's not a mental health professional

00:41:54.840 --> 00:41:57.410
to help the students
identify that as well,

00:41:57.410 --> 00:41:58.607
because that's something that students

00:41:58.607 --> 00:42:00.640
and all of us can struggle with at times,

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:03.170
when there is that mental
anguish or something that happens

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:05.390
in the moment and really
trying to distinguish

00:42:05.390 --> 00:42:06.340
between those 'cause I think...

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:07.960
And this has come up in
other episodes before,

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:10.857
there has been healthier
language around mental health,

00:42:10.857 --> 00:42:14.350
but sometimes I think
it does get confusing.

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:17.790
So even we had Victoria Garrett
who's a former D1 athlete

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:19.730
trying to really encourage
people when they say

00:42:19.730 --> 00:42:21.000
that they're feeling depressed,

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:22.270
but then also trying to think about,

00:42:22.270 --> 00:42:24.100
well, are they really depressed

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:25.740
or is it something in the moment?

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:27.580
And is it something to have...

00:42:27.580 --> 00:42:29.450
Always need to have the
tools to walk through those

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:32.050
versus trying to pull
in and get extra help.

00:42:32.050 --> 00:42:33.990
So the fact that the students
are aligning to be frank

00:42:33.990 --> 00:42:36.800
in those conversations, I
think it's really healthy

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:39.490
in a lot of ways and very encouraging.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:40.800
- Yeah, I think it is.

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:43.980
And I think we've seen this
also from the Olympic athletes

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:46.740
who are naming what they're going through

00:42:46.740 --> 00:42:51.250
and normalizing that
and naming their needs

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:53.460
and normalizing the language in such a way

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:56.157
that I think young people
will look at that and go,

00:42:56.157 --> 00:42:59.947
"Oh, that isn't quite
what I thought it was."

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.060
And they're okay.

00:43:02.060 --> 00:43:04.953
And other things happened
and that's okay too.

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:08.160
I think all of that is to the good.

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:08.993
- Yeah, definitely.

00:43:08.993 --> 00:43:11.300
And then as you mentioned,
having the openness to say,

00:43:11.300 --> 00:43:13.840
okay, you can walk them
down to a mental health

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:16.290
and counseling and there's not shame

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:17.273
that should be associated with that,

00:43:17.273 --> 00:43:19.030
that it's just part of the process.

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:21.413
And we should all be thinking
about getting the help

00:43:21.413 --> 00:43:23.410
that we need for whatever
we might be going through

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:25.040
at any specific point in time,

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:26.970
whether it's just the
anguish of a situation

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:29.080
or something that's
much more all consuming.

00:43:29.080 --> 00:43:31.760
So that's encouraging
for me to hear as well.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.320
- Yeah.
- Definitely.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:35.350
Omer, anything that you've run into

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:38.810
or noticed with the students
that you've been working with?

00:43:38.810 --> 00:43:39.643
- Sure.

00:43:39.643 --> 00:43:42.250
And thank you, Sharon for setting that up.

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:44.460
In fact, one of the things
that I wanna talk about,

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.750
which was related to that
is there was a new study

00:43:46.750 --> 00:43:49.370
that was just published in
JAMA Psychiatry last week

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:50.290
that got a lot of press.

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:52.400
NPR did a story on it,
and it got national press.

00:43:52.400 --> 00:43:55.340
It was out of Stanford and
really quickly the summary of it,

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:58.320
and it was very relevant
to this is that they found

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:01.260
that the American Muslim
community is twice as likely

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:02.920
to have attempted suicide

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.150
as compared to other
religious communities.

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:07.700
And then it was a
fascinating study and article

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:08.750
that delved into the reasons.

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:10.360
They said there's really
two factors, right?

00:44:10.360 --> 00:44:12.480
One is religious discrimination,

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:17.190
the toxic, corrosive effects
of Islamophobia and xenophobia

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:18.110
on these communities.

00:44:18.110 --> 00:44:19.510
And the second is community stigma.

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:21.590
And that's where I wanna connect
with what Sharon was saying

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:25.270
is that, all of our communities
in their respective ways

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:28.040
have had stigmas against mental
health and talking frankly

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:29.020
about these conversations.

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:30.940
And I think one thing
that has happened now

00:44:30.940 --> 00:44:32.690
is that our community
is, at least my community

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:34.120
in particular, I'll just talk about this,

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:36.170
is finding the door is opening, right?

00:44:36.170 --> 00:44:41.170
Where we're reaching crisis
levels of the corrosive effects

00:44:42.170 --> 00:44:43.330
of this stigma.

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:45.060
And so we need to start talking about this

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:46.540
and I really wanna applaud the people

00:44:46.540 --> 00:44:47.373
who participate in this.

00:44:47.373 --> 00:44:50.580
I know the director that
ran the study at Stanford,

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:54.290
wonderful female scholar, Dr. Rania Awaad,

00:44:54.290 --> 00:44:58.640
and just really from our
community and then looking outward

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:00.810
is pulling the curtain
back on these stigmas

00:45:01.839 --> 00:45:03.140
because it's something
that our students, right,

00:45:03.140 --> 00:45:03.973
and connect to the...

00:45:03.973 --> 00:45:05.960
The second thing I want to say is that,

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:10.020
for the longest time, our
students haven't wanted

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:12.210
to talk about these things,
but maybe it's just something

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:13.360
with the pandemic,

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:16.370
is it opened up conversation doors, right?

00:45:16.370 --> 00:45:17.873
And one of the things,
and to Sharon's point

00:45:17.873 --> 00:45:19.440
about just being frank,

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:20.740
I really love the way you framed that,

00:45:20.740 --> 00:45:21.810
is I wanna say this;

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:26.290
is that we understand Yale
is a high octane place.

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:27.790
The type of people that come to Yale,

00:45:27.790 --> 00:45:29.960
the type of people that excel at Yale,

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:31.290
it's all about excellence.

00:45:31.290 --> 00:45:34.500
It's a lot being the best
from the faculty to the staff,

00:45:34.500 --> 00:45:36.790
to the students, to the athletes and on.

00:45:36.790 --> 00:45:39.930
And so it becomes actually,
I think, very problematic

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:43.350
in that, that coupled with
this idea of the grind

00:45:43.350 --> 00:45:44.670
and hustle culture, right?

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:47.893
Is that like, what am I doing
on my Tik Tok and my IG?

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:50.270
How is it part of my brand

00:45:50.270 --> 00:45:52.420
and furthering my place in the world?

00:45:52.420 --> 00:45:54.710
And I think our students are
really susceptible to that.

00:45:54.710 --> 00:45:57.800
And some of the frank conversations
I had with my students

00:45:57.800 --> 00:46:01.110
is like, "That's not our real..."

00:46:01.110 --> 00:46:04.250
We all need to learn to
dial it down and to be real.

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:07.100
There's a performativeness
that is almost fetishized,

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:09.010
I think, amongst our society now,

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:12.500
and especially at these high
pressure cookers like Yale

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:14.250
and that, I think, one of the...

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:15.950
It's a negative, but it's a breakthrough

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:17.560
that I think I had with some students

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:19.300
or that students have
themselves, I should say,

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:22.973
is that they're just like,
yeah, that's not sustainable.

00:46:23.993 --> 00:46:25.570
That's not gonna be sustainable
through the pandemic.

00:46:25.570 --> 00:46:27.750
And I don't know if it's gonna
be sustainable afterward.

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:32.750
So, if they're able from
that negative experience

00:46:33.350 --> 00:46:37.080
to walk and to say that
I need to dial it down,

00:46:37.080 --> 00:46:39.250
hey, that's a win, right?

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:42.190
So yeah, that's something I put out there.

00:46:42.190 --> 00:46:43.023
- That's great.

00:46:43.023 --> 00:46:43.856
That's great.

00:46:43.856 --> 00:46:45.540
And something that I
think is still applicable,

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:49.280
even in the broader sense,
and as you're talking about

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:53.090
I'm thinking if that framework
can perpetuate how powerful

00:46:53.090 --> 00:46:56.510
that can be to really just
shift things across culture.

00:46:56.510 --> 00:46:59.400
And I'm curious to hear that
you've had those conversations

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:03.350
in a lot of ways and the
openness is definitely important.

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:06.580
And even as, I mean, you
mentioned the first challenge

00:47:06.580 --> 00:47:08.960
with all the Islamophobia and xenophobia

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:11.150
that obviously still has to be addressed,

00:47:11.150 --> 00:47:14.670
but I'm encouraged that the
conversation is happening

00:47:14.670 --> 00:47:18.280
and those other pieces are
much bigger conversations

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:21.120
than we're having here,
but it'd be great to see,

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:24.350
on both sides, if we can really
do that across the board.

00:47:24.350 --> 00:47:26.920
But again, I feel like
I'm repeating myself.

00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:28.910
I'm grateful for just the
way that you all are engaging

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.570
with students and just
all that's coming out

00:47:30.570 --> 00:47:32.060
of that already, which I think

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:34.110
is really powerful and impactful.

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:36.990
Orlando, anything you wanted
to add in your interactions

00:47:36.990 --> 00:47:40.530
with your students in your
sphere of influence as well?

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:45.530
- Yes, I think one kind
of misunderstanding

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:51.500
or even missed opportunity
is in recognizing the overlap

00:47:51.500 --> 00:47:55.510
between community care in health

00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:58.020
and individual care and health,

00:47:58.020 --> 00:48:00.280
as it relates to mental health.

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:04.860
And students, in my experience,
are more open to speaking

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:07.550
about what's happening
for them individually

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:10.170
and requesting things individually.

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:12.840
But students won't necessarily
talk with each other

00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:16.750
as much as I think they could
benefit if there was more

00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:20.810
of a community of support
in a community of care.

00:48:20.810 --> 00:48:25.590
And one of my challenges is
saying, okay, how can I support

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:28.110
what's happening at the individual level

00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:33.070
and in an appropriate way, and
in a way that can be healing

00:48:33.070 --> 00:48:36.260
to allow the community
aspect to come together.

00:48:36.260 --> 00:48:37.650
So that's something that I'm working on.

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:40.990
So I think there seems to be not a stigma

00:48:40.990 --> 00:48:43.580
with being transparent one on one,

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:47.180
but perhaps still a stigma
with being transparent

00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:49.570
in more of a group setting.

00:48:49.570 --> 00:48:51.780
So that's one thing that I'm experiencing

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:52.850
and thinking about.

00:48:52.850 --> 00:48:56.490
And the other piece is,
with students do want help,

00:48:56.490 --> 00:49:00.210
they're asking me to help
them to find specialists

00:49:02.020 --> 00:49:06.244
who look like them and have
their faith background,

00:49:06.244 --> 00:49:10.170
or that even as they're going
through a treatment process

00:49:10.170 --> 00:49:14.337
or care process, they
are not directed away

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:17.203
from their values.

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:22.500
And so that has presented
me with an opportunity

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:26.780
to try to see, okay, who are
individuals who may relate

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:31.650
with students and do
some vetting in a way.

00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:34.430
And I found that it is not easy.

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:35.263
It is not easy.

00:49:35.263 --> 00:49:39.290
Like I don't have a huge
large pool to choose from.

00:49:39.290 --> 00:49:43.820
What does help is the
opportunity of virtual support?

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:47.250
So maybe there's a larger
network than just who was in,

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:50.010
let's say New Haven or Connecticut,

00:49:50.010 --> 00:49:54.190
but might be able to connect
students with professionals

00:49:54.190 --> 00:49:57.440
who were in their own
hometown or home country

00:49:57.440 --> 00:49:58.883
and to be able to do that.

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:02.090
So I think those are two areas,

00:50:02.090 --> 00:50:06.640
be more of a community support
and having mental health

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:09.280
to be a community process and focus,

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:13.670
and then also helping students to be okay

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:15.340
with who I walked them to

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:18.610
in terms of getting
more professional help.

00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:20.540
And those are two areas
that I need to grow in,

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:22.970
that I'm trying to train in right now,

00:50:22.970 --> 00:50:26.510
so that I'm better equipped
and I have more of a network

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:29.740
of professionals around
me as we dive into this

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:32.240
for this particular year and beyond.

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:33.073
- Yeah.

00:50:33.073 --> 00:50:34.410
Yeah, that's really important
and I'm glad you brought that

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:36.120
to the forefront as well.

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:38.450
Something that the field has
been grappling with for a while

00:50:38.450 --> 00:50:41.290
in a lot of ways is just
making sure that people are...

00:50:41.290 --> 00:50:44.320
The phrase people use is
to have cultural competency

00:50:44.320 --> 00:50:46.560
in terms of what background they have

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:47.580
and even the faith traditions.

00:50:47.580 --> 00:50:50.130
I mean, I think the mental
health field has moved forward

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:51.360
in a lot of ways.

00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:53.010
I think and Sharon, you
mentioned this before,

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:54.750
even within the mental health field,

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:57.800
I think in the past there was
a tendency to be dismissive

00:50:57.800 --> 00:51:00.780
about people's cultural
experiences and to be dismissive

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:02.300
about the faith journeys.

00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:04.100
Whereas now, there is
more of a push to say,

00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:06.860
no, we really need to think
about people as whole people

00:51:06.860 --> 00:51:09.740
and not pretend that
those things aren't part

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:12.220
of people's lives and that portends

00:51:12.220 --> 00:51:15.530
that even despite all the
research and the evidence,

00:51:15.530 --> 00:51:17.110
so end up being members of communities

00:51:17.110 --> 00:51:18.650
and faith communities in general,

00:51:18.650 --> 00:51:21.090
it can have both positive
and negative effects

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:22.040
on people's mental health,

00:51:22.040 --> 00:51:24.060
depending on how that's approached.

00:51:24.060 --> 00:51:27.767
But you can't just dismiss the
whole thing and remove that.

00:51:27.767 --> 00:51:31.260
And as people of faith here
who believe in the power of bad

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:32.240
to work through these things,

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:33.630
that needs to be integral as well.

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:36.720
But to your point, Orlando,
it's still difficult for people

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:38.490
to get connected with the resources

00:51:38.490 --> 00:51:40.680
because the resources
aren't what they need to be.

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:43.230
So kudos to you for doing that homework.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:45.120
But in some ways, it also
reflects on the system

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:46.533
that you have to do that
homework in the first place,

00:51:46.533 --> 00:51:49.290
and that's not readily available.

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:52.893
That's something we're still
definitely moving forward with.

00:51:53.745 --> 00:51:56.780
And just to say, if your students
want access to resources,

00:51:56.780 --> 00:51:57.810
they can definitely reach out to me

00:51:57.810 --> 00:51:59.900
and I can put them in touch with folks.

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:01.630
Several of the guests who
have come on this podcast

00:52:01.630 --> 00:52:02.950
have focused on that in particular.

00:52:02.950 --> 00:52:05.810
And so I think it's an ongoing
and important conversation.

00:52:05.810 --> 00:52:08.690
Again, just appreciate your
transparency in sharing that

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:11.170
'cause it's really, really
important and critical.

00:52:11.170 --> 00:52:12.710
And again, I keep harping back to this,

00:52:12.710 --> 00:52:14.900
but you all are on the
front lines in so many ways

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:19.070
and really encouraging and
empowering the students

00:52:19.070 --> 00:52:22.130
in those conversations and
normalizing it as well.

00:52:22.130 --> 00:52:24.330
I know we used normalizing
in different ways,

00:52:24.330 --> 00:52:26.320
we can't get back to
normal that we had before,

00:52:26.320 --> 00:52:28.607
but we can continue to
normalize these conversations.

00:52:28.607 --> 00:52:32.800
And it sounds like you all are
doing that in so many ways.

00:52:32.800 --> 00:52:35.390
The time always goes fast on this podcast,

00:52:35.390 --> 00:52:38.330
but I did want to wrap up just
by talking about the vision

00:52:38.330 --> 00:52:41.000
that you all have for
the year going forward.

00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:43.710
And vision in a sense of hopes and dreams.

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:46.400
Maybe not in the sense
of specific strategies,

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:49.500
'cause we've already made
sure that that's not agnostic,

00:52:49.500 --> 00:52:51.920
that that's not the case here.

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:54.900
But yeah, just your vision for yourselves

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:57.740
and for the students
as they're coming back.

00:52:57.740 --> 00:52:59.540
And not to throw too many
questions in at once,

00:52:59.540 --> 00:53:01.340
but as we've been talking,
I've also been thinking

00:53:01.340 --> 00:53:04.520
about all three of you and just
your own emotional wellness

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:07.030
as you are carrying and walking
through with the students.

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:09.940
And that can be a load
and a burden sometimes,

00:53:09.940 --> 00:53:11.230
not that it's meant to be a burden,

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:14.230
but just hoping that
you all have this space

00:53:14.230 --> 00:53:16.010
for your own self care as well.

00:53:16.010 --> 00:53:17.490
And it sounds like you
have that in community,

00:53:17.490 --> 00:53:20.830
but I'm just curious, as
you talk about vision,

00:53:20.830 --> 00:53:23.640
if there's space for you to
even talk about how you envision

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:24.980
that for yourself as well.

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:28.210
So that was a very loaded
and very compound question.

00:53:28.210 --> 00:53:30.900
So I feel bad calling
on anyone to go first,

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:32.790
but I'm going to anyway.

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:35.743
Omer, would you be willing to jump in?

00:53:37.040 --> 00:53:39.300
- No, I really appreciate
the questions in that.

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:40.780
And so what I would say is two things.

00:53:40.780 --> 00:53:45.780
One is that just the return to community

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:48.650
and to just be honest about
what that's going to look like,

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:50.310
that we're gonna phase it in,

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:52.450
but the goal is ultimately, God willing,

00:53:52.450 --> 00:53:55.253
to move to a more connected community.

00:53:56.300 --> 00:53:58.170
I think that we can be authentic

00:53:58.170 --> 00:54:00.130
and honest with our students, right?

00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:03.160
With our humanities, not just students.

00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:05.300
Because that way we manage expectations.

00:54:05.300 --> 00:54:08.950
So my vision is that we're
gonna emerge from this wizened

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:12.810
and in many ways with
a deeper appreciation

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:15.560
for the things that we missed,
that we've missed out on

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:18.250
that we lost in one sense.

00:54:18.250 --> 00:54:19.820
And then in terms of the other thing

00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:23.053
that I would say is I think that...

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:26.723
There's a saying, right,

00:54:26.723 --> 00:54:28.120
every chapel would need the chaplain.

00:54:28.120 --> 00:54:32.780
And so I am so deeply grateful
for Sharon and Orlando

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:34.930
and yourself and just
friends and colleagues

00:54:34.930 --> 00:54:37.480
that are in these spaces
that do this work,

00:54:37.480 --> 00:54:39.750
that at least for me helped me process it

00:54:39.750 --> 00:54:43.300
because I am an obsessive reader of news.

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:46.000
Unfortunately, I'm a news junkie.

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:50.440
And the world is a very
difficult, in many ways,

00:54:50.440 --> 00:54:54.057
heartbreaking place and
if we get tunnel vision

00:54:54.057 --> 00:54:57.170
as you get sucked into that,
it is so disheartening.

00:54:57.170 --> 00:55:00.670
So I think the ability to be
able to process it with friends

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:04.430
and loved ones and to just
really talk it through,

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:07.230
that to me is just like
been a saving grace.

00:55:07.230 --> 00:55:09.010
And I think helps us keep all our sanity

00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:12.460
and our sort of mental
health and spiritual health.

00:55:12.460 --> 00:55:13.630
- Agreed. Agreed.

00:55:13.630 --> 00:55:15.160
Well, I appreciate you
talking that through

00:55:15.160 --> 00:55:18.170
with us today too 'cause
it's been encouraging

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:20.130
and in some ways, eye opening
for me as well, just to hear,

00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:21.180
again, just the pieces

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:23.850
that you all have been walking
through with your students.

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:25.050
Orlando, what about you?

00:55:26.340 --> 00:55:28.060
- Yes, I'm thinking about two things.

00:55:28.060 --> 00:55:31.863
One, values and gifts.

00:55:32.750 --> 00:55:36.240
So in terms of values, what
are our community values

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:39.692
that we can lean into at this time?

00:55:39.692 --> 00:55:42.120
And what are I individual values

00:55:42.120 --> 00:55:44.430
that I can help encourage students

00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:46.070
to lean in to at this time?

00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:47.550
So even if it's not...

00:55:48.770 --> 00:55:50.900
Even if a strategy is not quite clear

00:55:50.900 --> 00:55:55.900
or a program is not quite
clear, we can have a matrix

00:55:57.050 --> 00:55:59.550
upon which or through
which we make decisions.

00:55:59.550 --> 00:56:03.380
And that matrix can be our
collective and individual values.

00:56:03.380 --> 00:56:07.357
Now, the challenge is,
with every new semester

00:56:07.357 --> 00:56:09.430
and with every new school year,

00:56:09.430 --> 00:56:13.120
the population of students changes.

00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:16.490
And with that, there may
come differences in values

00:56:16.490 --> 00:56:18.950
or maybe not, but I think I'm
still gonna have to go through

00:56:18.950 --> 00:56:22.580
the process of saying, "Okay,
well, what are our values

00:56:22.580 --> 00:56:24.320
and how can lean into them?"

00:56:24.320 --> 00:56:27.460
One, so that we are sure where we stand,

00:56:27.460 --> 00:56:31.320
but also so that we can let
other people know where we stand

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:35.170
and be more of an inviting community.

00:56:35.170 --> 00:56:38.990
And then that will allow
us, especially on my end,

00:56:38.990 --> 00:56:41.963
to have values-based collaboration.

00:56:43.180 --> 00:56:46.400
So for Orlando to do
what Orlando can do well,

00:56:46.400 --> 00:56:50.000
and then for me to broaden
the network of who our members

00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:54.380
and students can reach out
to and be in community with.

00:56:54.380 --> 00:56:55.880
So that community...

00:56:55.880 --> 00:56:59.160
One blessing of the pandemic
is that our identity

00:56:59.160 --> 00:57:03.720
is not tied to a place, is not
tied to a physical location,

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:05.280
is not tied to an address,

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:07.350
it's not even tied to a time zone.

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:11.440
So how can we think more
broadly and more inclusively

00:57:11.440 --> 00:57:13.250
about who we are and who we can be

00:57:13.250 --> 00:57:15.340
in relationship to one another?

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:19.680
And if we can start to identify

00:57:21.100 --> 00:57:22.930
and choose what values we want,

00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:25.010
I think that will help with collaboration

00:57:25.010 --> 00:57:27.180
and deepening community.

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:29.503
And then the other
piece in terms of gifts,

00:57:33.250 --> 00:57:35.370
I think there's healing in the body.

00:57:35.370 --> 00:57:40.370
And I think that everything
we need is in the house.

00:57:40.550 --> 00:57:42.060
And I think about God's house.

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:44.250
But I think there are enough gifts.

00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:46.640
I think that there are enough strengths

00:57:46.640 --> 00:57:48.400
in who we are collectively

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:53.620
that when any one of us
leans into those strengths,

00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:58.620
but at the same time, we
really can bring a lot of hope,

00:57:59.090 --> 00:58:00.520
bring a lot of healing,

00:58:00.520 --> 00:58:03.040
bring a lot of positive transformation

00:58:03.040 --> 00:58:05.850
to everyone with whom we are connected.

00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:10.230
I know one of my gifts is
exhortation and exhortation

00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:13.810
is a time for now or now is
the time for exhortation.

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:16.960
So I'm trying to say, "Okay,
how can I lean into who I am?

00:58:16.960 --> 00:58:19.290
And for people who have other strengths,

00:58:19.290 --> 00:58:22.250
how can I encourage them to
lean in to those strengths?"

00:58:22.250 --> 00:58:25.663
And BCAY is a student-run
church and students,

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:29.130
as they're young adults beginning,

00:58:29.130 --> 00:58:33.250
they don't always recognize
what their strengths are,

00:58:33.250 --> 00:58:36.470
what their gifts are, and what
the gem is that they bring

00:58:36.470 --> 00:58:39.000
to their families or those around them.

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:41.823
And some who do recognize
it, don't believe it.

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:44.930
And a question I have is, Orlando, okay,

00:58:44.930 --> 00:58:48.800
how can I help raise
for them the opportunity

00:58:48.800 --> 00:58:53.230
to see that not only are they
a part of a larger community,

00:58:53.230 --> 00:58:56.690
but they are a valuable
part of a larger community?

00:58:56.690 --> 00:58:59.500
And not based on how they perform,

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:02.320
but based on who they are at
their core and what they value

00:59:02.320 --> 00:59:04.080
and what they see.

00:59:04.080 --> 00:59:07.500
And I think that's a challenge
that I wanna lean into

00:59:07.500 --> 00:59:09.190
as we go into this year,

00:59:09.190 --> 00:59:14.190
to celebrate gifts of what
people bring to the table

00:59:14.490 --> 00:59:17.020
and also to lift up values

00:59:17.020 --> 00:59:19.260
to see if we can be more collaborative

00:59:19.260 --> 00:59:22.750
and expand our definition of community.

00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:23.820
- That's so good.

00:59:23.820 --> 00:59:25.300
So many important pieces there.

00:59:25.300 --> 00:59:27.170
I mean, just the value component as well,

00:59:27.170 --> 00:59:29.110
and the strengths that students can bring

00:59:29.110 --> 00:59:30.470
to encourage one another.

00:59:30.470 --> 00:59:32.360
And in some ways, I'm speculating
here, but I have a sense

00:59:32.360 --> 00:59:35.230
that that's something
that you all are doing

00:59:35.230 --> 00:59:37.270
in your role in the
chaplain's office as well,

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:39.250
even the way you talked
about leaning on one another,

00:59:39.250 --> 00:59:41.520
people stepping in,
that people have a sense

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:44.520
of their values and their
strengths and their gems,

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:48.610
and are able to use those
to encourage the whole body

00:59:48.610 --> 00:59:49.980
within the chaplains organization.

00:59:49.980 --> 00:59:51.810
So to see that amongst
your students as well,

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:53.200
goes such a long way.

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:54.730
And even from, I'm thinking just in terms

00:59:54.730 --> 00:59:56.190
of a mental health perspective,

00:59:56.190 --> 00:59:57.880
some of the mental health interventions,

00:59:57.880 --> 01:00:00.053
they're really focused on perspective.

01:00:01.160 --> 01:00:03.080
What is actually happening in the world

01:00:03.080 --> 01:00:06.550
versus how do I perceive
that and really shifting that

01:00:06.550 --> 01:00:08.760
to make sure that
perceptions master reality.

01:00:08.760 --> 01:00:10.690
And so if the students are getting a sense

01:00:10.690 --> 01:00:12.600
of their true value in
having that perception,

01:00:12.600 --> 01:00:14.917
how powerful that can
be to moving forward,

01:00:14.917 --> 01:00:18.080
not that it solves everything,
but having that appropriate

01:00:18.080 --> 01:00:21.040
and fixed perspective in the
same way that we can do that

01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:23.150
through reading holy scriptures
and things like that,

01:00:23.150 --> 01:00:25.620
to reshape our perspective,
what does God say about us

01:00:25.620 --> 01:00:28.060
and how do we use that to
then interact with the world

01:00:28.060 --> 01:00:28.970
and think about ourselves?

01:00:28.970 --> 01:00:31.700
So, yeah, just encouraged by the framing

01:00:31.700 --> 01:00:34.550
that you've put there and for
all that you all are doing.

01:00:35.570 --> 01:00:38.170
Sharon, to give you the last
word, to wrap all this together

01:00:38.170 --> 01:00:41.990
with these great comments
from Omer and the Orlando.

01:00:41.990 --> 01:00:45.693
- Well, I'm humbled by my
beloved preaching brothers.

01:00:45.693 --> 01:00:47.943
(laughing)

01:00:50.076 --> 01:00:52.600
I won't be anywhere near as eloquent,

01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:57.410
but I will honestly say my
vision is blurry for the future,

01:00:57.410 --> 01:00:58.880
but I am fueled by grace.

01:00:58.880 --> 01:01:00.270
- Honest.
- That I know.

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:02.850
I have an abiding faith
that that is present

01:01:04.541 --> 01:01:09.541
and I hope to embody it in forms
of compassion and kindness,

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:14.840
and how to sustain that for ourselves

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:19.450
so that we can continue to
do this work really has to do

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:23.810
with, in many ways, knowing your limits,

01:01:23.810 --> 01:01:27.530
knowing that you are called to be present,

01:01:27.530 --> 01:01:29.263
but you aren't called to fix.

01:01:30.230 --> 01:01:35.230
And so to not look away,
to lead with kindness

01:01:35.350 --> 01:01:39.670
and compassion and know that the rest

01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:41.363
is really out of our hands.

01:01:42.290 --> 01:01:46.440
But I feel grateful for my colleagues.

01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:48.403
I feel grateful for my family.

01:01:50.322 --> 01:01:53.320
And I know that (laughing)
the impact of this work

01:01:55.760 --> 01:01:59.240
is something we won't see (laughing)

01:02:00.200 --> 01:02:03.123
and that I came to terms
with that years ago.

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:08.330
And there's a lightness to
that that I think is healthy

01:02:08.330 --> 01:02:10.500
to keep in mind, especially
at a place like Yale,

01:02:10.500 --> 01:02:12.460
where there's lots of talk about legacy

01:02:12.460 --> 01:02:15.620
and impact and all these things.

01:02:15.620 --> 01:02:17.950
I can't do the work with that in mind.

01:02:17.950 --> 01:02:22.950
I do the work the present and
hope that those seeds lead

01:02:23.330 --> 01:02:25.393
to a better world.

01:02:26.260 --> 01:02:27.363
- Such good wisdom.

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:30.470
- Agreed. Agreed.

01:02:30.470 --> 01:02:32.317
Well, thank you to all of you
for this rich conversations,

01:02:32.317 --> 01:02:34.580
it was very edifying in a lot of ways.

01:02:34.580 --> 01:02:36.600
Even as we're walking through
these challenging times,

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:37.730
even with the uncertainties

01:02:37.730 --> 01:02:39.547
to know just the power of community

01:02:39.547 --> 01:02:42.150
and the power of knowing our
value and knowing our worth

01:02:42.150 --> 01:02:45.060
and having others who are
walking through that with us,

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:47.680
I think goes so far and it'll be important

01:02:47.680 --> 01:02:50.250
for all of us across the
country as we're heading back

01:02:50.250 --> 01:02:55.250
into this new semester with
so many different aspects

01:02:55.310 --> 01:02:56.820
of what we haven't experienced before.

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:59.270
So again, grateful to the
work that you all are doing,

01:02:59.270 --> 01:03:00.250
grateful for your willingness

01:03:00.250 --> 01:03:01.410
to share this with our audience.

01:03:01.410 --> 01:03:03.500
I know it's gonna be something
that will be enriching

01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:05.300
and a blessing to them as well.

01:03:05.300 --> 01:03:07.380
Thank you again for taking
time out of your schedules,

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:09.040
even as you're getting
close to the semester start

01:03:09.040 --> 01:03:10.560
to come here and have this conversation.

01:03:10.560 --> 01:03:11.793
I'm deeply appreciative.

01:03:12.919 --> 01:03:14.480
- Okay, thank you.
- Thank you.

01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:16.460
Thank you so much, Nii.

01:03:16.460 --> 01:03:17.668
- Thank you, Nii.

01:03:17.668 --> 01:03:20.251
(upbeat music)

